      <div class="level-banner" id="level-3">
        <div class="level-tag">Level 3 · Complete technical documentation</div>
        <h2 class="level-heading">Explore the mathematics</h2>
        <p class="level-dek">
          Every rule, threshold, equation, coefficient and worked example — open only the sections
          you want to inspect.
        </p>
      </div>

      <section class="band band-lead" id="math">
        <nav class="math-index" aria-label="Technical sections">
          <a class="math-link" href="#eligibility">Eligibility &amp; minutes</a>
          <a class="math-link" href="#opponents">Opponent model</a>
          <a class="math-link" href="#shooting">Shooting formulas</a>
          <a class="math-link" href="#defence">Team defence badge</a>
          <a class="math-link" href="#examples">Worked examples</a>
          <a class="math-link" href="#sheet">Player sheet details</a>
          <a class="math-link" href="#reliability">Reliability</a>
          <a class="math-link" href="#when">When set</a>
        </nav>

        {/* 3.1 Eligibility, positions and minutes */}
        <details class="tech-disclosure" id="eligibility">
          <summary>
            <span>
              1. Eligibility, positions &amp; playing time
              <span class="tech-summary-meta">Minimum minutes, team-share bars, position rules &amp; minutes-of-record priority</span>
            </span>
          </summary>
          <div class="tech-body">
            <div class="table-wrap">
              <table class="who">
                <thead>
                  <tr>
                    <th scope="col"><span class="vh">Rule</span></th>
                    <th scope="col">Shooting and scoring</th>
                    <th scope="col">Team figures on a sheet</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr>
                    <th scope="row">Positions</th>
                    <td>Forwards (F) and midfielders (M), compared separately</td>
                    <td>Defenders (D) and goalkeepers (GK)</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <th scope="row">Tiers</th>
                    <td>Tier 1: top 5% (rounded up + ties). Tier 2: next 15% (to top 20% + ties).</td>
                    <td>None. The team defence badge is the team's, shown on the team page.</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <th scope="row">Eligibility</th>
                    <td>
                      900 minutes this season, or 600 minutes in each of the last two seasons as F
                      or M at the same programme.
                    </td>
                    <td>
                      A regular this season (thresholds below), at a programme with at least 12
                      complete team matches.
                    </td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <th scope="row">Seasons counted</th>
                    <td>This season, plus last season if it had 600+ minutes as F or M.</td>
                    <td>This season alone.</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>

            <h4 class="subhead">Defensive regular thresholds</h4>
            <p class="para">
              A defender's or goalkeeper's sheet carries the team's figures only when the player
              clears both a playing-time floor and a minimum share of the team's recorded minutes (<span class="sym">minutes ÷ (matches × 90)</span>):
            </p>
            <ul class="list">
              <li><strong>Defender:</strong> at least <span class="sym">900</span> minutes and at least <span class="sym">70%</span> of the team's minutes.</li>
              <li><strong>Goalkeeper:</strong> at least <span class="sym">600</span> minutes and at least <span class="sym">50%</span> of the team's minutes.</li>
            </ul>

            <h4 class="subhead">Position assignment</h4>
            <p class="para">
              Positions come from the roster. When a roster lists two position groups (such as
              “F/M” or “MF-D”), Touchline checks the player's box-score lines that season: if one
              of those listed groups is the most common and appears at least <strong>three times</strong>,
              that group wins (ties go to the earliest date). Otherwise the roster's primary
              reading stands. When a roster lists no position, Touchline uses the most common
              box-score group (ties to earliest date).
            </p>

            <h4 class="subhead">Minutes of record</h4>
            <p class="para">
              Because some programmes omit player minutes in certain box scores, Touchline selects
              a player's minutes in this exact order:
            </p>
            <ol class="list">
              <li>
                <strong>All team box scores print minutes:</strong> sum of box-score minutes.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Some box scores omit minutes, but a season stats total is published:</strong>{" "}
                the larger of the stats-page total and the box-score sum (printed minutes act as a
                floor). For goalkeepers, both the field-player and goalkeeping tables are checked
                and the higher total is used.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Some box scores omit minutes and no stats total is published:</strong>{" "}
                box-score minutes are used if at least <strong>half</strong> of the team's matches
                print minutes. If a player falls short of eligibility on printed minutes, Touchline
                checks whether adding <span class="sym">90</span> minutes for each unprinted match
                they appeared in (or where no team lines were printed) would push them over the
                bar; if it could, the player is marked <strong>Incomplete records</strong> instead
                of <strong>Needs more minutes</strong>.
              </li>
            </ol>
            <p class="para">
              Because stats pages and match box scores can differ — and tiers freeze once a season
              — the minutes beside a tier can differ from the season total elsewhere on the sheet.
              Whenever they differ, the sheet states where the tier's count came from.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>

        {/* 3.2 Opponent strength and venue model */}
        <details class="tech-disclosure" id="opponents">
          <summary>
            <span>
              2. Opponent strength &amp; home-field model
              <span class="tech-summary-meta">Multiplicative goals and shots models fitted jointly across all collected seasons</span>
            </span>
          </summary>
          <div class="tech-body">
            <p class="para">
              Touchline fits two multiplicative team-strength models across all collected seasons
              (men and women separately): one for <strong>goals</strong> and one for{" "}
              <strong>shots</strong>. Every team-season receives an attack factor (
              <span class="sym">att</span>) and a defence factor (<span class="sym">dfn</span>),
              centred around <span class="sym">1.0</span> for an average team:
            </p>
            <div class="formula">
              <div class="formula-eq">
                Expected in a match = μ × att[attacking team] × dfn[defending team] × venue
              </div>
              <div class="formula-note">
                <strong>μ</strong> is the division-wide average per team per match (~1.5–1.6 goals,
                ~12.5 shots). <strong>venue</strong> is <strong>h</strong> at home (~1.10 for
                goals, ~1.07–1.08 for shots), <strong>1 ÷ h</strong> away, and{" "}
                <strong>1.0</strong> at a neutral site.
              </div>
            </div>
            <p class="para">
              Solved over 40 fixed-point passes with regularizing priors of{" "}
              <span class="sym">6.0</span> goals and <span class="sym">40.0</span> shots pulling
              small samples toward <span class="sym">1.0</span>. Because every team updates against
              its actual opponents on every pass, strength of schedule is built into every factor.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>

        {/* 3.3 Shooting and scoring formulas */}
        <details class="tech-disclosure" id="shooting">
          <summary>
            <span>
              3. Shooting &amp; scoring formulas
              <span class="tech-summary-meta">Penalty removal, per-90 threat weights, multi-season blending &amp; percentile cutoffs</span>
            </span>
          </summary>
          <div class="tech-body">
            <ol class="steps">
              <li class="step">
                <div class="step-body">
                  <h4 class="step-title">Strip penalties and adjust each match</h4>
                  <p class="para">
                    Penalty goals and attempts (read from play-by-play and cross-checked against
                    the season stats file; if the stats file reports extra penalties, season
                    adjusted totals are scaled down proportionally) are removed. Each match's
                    non-penalty goals (<span class="sym">g</span>) and shots (
                    <span class="sym">s</span>) are divided by the opponent's defence factor times
                    the venue factor, then summed over the season into <span class="sym">adjG</span>{" "}
                    and <span class="sym">adjS</span>:
                  </p>
                  <div class="formula">
                    <div class="formula-eq">
                      match adjG = g ÷ (dfn_goals[opponent] × venue_goals)<br />
                      match adjS = s ÷ (dfn_shots[opponent] × venue_shots)
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </li>

              <li class="step">
                <div class="step-body">
                  <h4 class="step-title">Compute per-90 threat rate</h4>
                  <p class="para">
                    Adjusted goals and shots are converted to per-90 rates and combined with fixed
                    weights (assists are not included):
                  </p>
                  <div class="formula">
                    <div class="formula-eq">
                      threat = wG × (adjG × 90 ÷ minutes) + wS × (adjS × 90 ÷ minutes)
                    </div>
                    <div class="formula-note">
                      <strong>Men:</strong> wG = 0.234, wS = 0.0885 (1 goal = 2.64 shots).{" "}
                      <strong>Women:</strong> wG = 0.238, wS = 0.0749 (1 goal = 3.18 shots).
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </li>

              <li class="step">
                <div class="step-body">
                  <h4 class="step-title">Blend with prior season and position-group average</h4>
                  <p class="para">
                    Each eligible player's threat rate is blended with the position-group average
                    (mean threat of all complete players in that position group and gender with at
                    least 600 minutes) and, when linked and qualified (600+ minutes as F or M at
                    the same programme), the prior season's threat:
                  </p>
                  <div class="formula">
                    <div class="formula-eq">
                      1 season: Men = 0.79 × current + 0.21 × avg · Women = 0.82 × current + 0.18 × avg<br />
                      2 seasons: Men = 0.59 × current + 0.34 × prior + 0.07 × avg · Women = 0.64 × current + 0.30 × prior + 0.06 × avg
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </li>

              <li class="step">
                <div class="step-body">
                  <h4 class="step-title">Apply percentage cutoffs with exact ties</h4>
                  <p class="para">
                    Within each gender and position group (forwards separately from midfielders),
                    all <span class="sym">N</span> eligible players are ordered by blended
                    estimate:
                  </p>
                  <div class="formula">
                    <div class="formula-eq">
                      Tier 1: top ceil(0.05 × N), plus exact ties at the cutoff<br />
                      Tier 2: remaining players inside top ceil(0.20 × N), plus exact ties at the cutoff
                    </div>
                    <div class="formula-note">
                      Once assigned, the numerical estimates and ordering are discarded and never
                      stored in the published tier files.
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </div>
        </details>

        {/* 3.4 Team defence formulas */}
        <details class="tech-disclosure" id="defence">
          <summary>
            <span>
              4. Team defence badge
              <span class="tech-summary-meta">Expected goals against, log-ratio record, top fifth of the division &amp; the workload label</span>
            </span>
          </summary>
          <div class="tech-body">
            <ol class="steps">
              <li class="step">
                <div class="step-body">
                  <h4 class="step-title">Total goals conceded against expected goals (12+ matches)</h4>
                  <p class="para">
                    Over all collected matches with complete integer goals and shots (a team needs
                    at least <span class="sym">12</span> matches to qualify), Touchline totals{" "}
                    <span class="sym">GA</span> (goals conceded), <span class="sym">SA</span>{" "}
                    (shots faced), and <span class="sym">EGA</span> (expected goals against):
                  </p>
                  <div class="formula">
                    <div class="formula-eq">
                      EGA = sum of (μ_goals × att_goals[opponent] ÷ venue_goals) across the team's matches
                    </div>
                    <div class="formula-note">
                      On player sheets, EGA is rounded and printed as “what its opponents usually score.”
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </li>

              <li class="step">
                <div class="step-body">
                  <h4 class="step-title">Rank across the whole division and award the top fifth</h4>
                  <p class="para">
                    Every qualifying team in the season (men and women separately) is ranked by its
                    record, and the top <span class="sym">ceil(0.20 × N)</span> teams, plus exact
                    ties, hold the <strong>team defence badge</strong> for that season. The badge is
                    one season's: it is not averaged with earlier seasons, and a team page lists the
                    earlier seasons a programme held it beside this one.
                  </p>
                </div>
              </li>

              <li class="step">
                <div class="step-body">
                  <h4 class="step-title">Label the workload, without ranking by it</h4>
                  <p class="para">
                    Each qualifying team's shots faced per match (<span class="sym">SA ÷ matches</span>)
                    places it in a third for that season and gender, at the 1/3 and 2/3
                    linear-interpolation quantiles: <strong>Light</strong>,{" "}
                    <strong>Typical</strong> or <strong>Heavy</strong>, a team on a boundary joining
                    the heavier. The label is printed beside the team's figures so a reader can see
                    how much defending it did; the badge does not compare teams only within a third.
                  </p>
                </div>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </div>
        </details>

        {/* 3.5 Worked mathematical examples */}
        <details class="tech-disclosure" id="examples">
          <summary>
            <span>
              5. Worked mathematical examples
              <span class="tech-summary-meta">Complete step-by-step arithmetic for one forward and two teams' defence (illustrative values)</span>
            </span>
          </summary>
          <div class="tech-body">
            <div class="example-head">Example A · Shooting and scoring (illustrative men's forward)</div>
            <p class="para">
              Suppose a men's forward plays <strong>1,350 minutes</strong> (15 full matches),
              recording 9 non-penalty goals and 42 non-penalty shots against slightly
              tougher-than-average defences, while the men's forward average threat is{" "}
              <strong>0.2800</strong>.
            </p>
            <ul class="example-rows">
              <li>
                <span class="example-label">1. Opponent-adjusted</span>
                <span>9.75 adjG and 45.0 adjS → <strong>0.65 adjG/90</strong> and <strong>3.00 adjS/90</strong></span>
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="example-label">2. Season threat</span>
                <span>0.234 × 0.65 + 0.0885 × 3.00 = 0.1521 + 0.2655 = <strong>0.4176</strong></span>
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="example-label">3a. If 1 season counted</span>
                <span>0.79 × 0.4176 + 0.21 × 0.2800 = <strong>0.389</strong></span>
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="example-label">3b. If 2 seasons counted</span>
                <span>With last season's threat at 0.3900: 0.59 × 0.4176 + 0.34 × 0.3900 + 0.07 × 0.2800 = <strong>0.399</strong></span>
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="example-label">4. Applying the cut</span>
                <span>Among 320 eligible men's forwards, the top 16 (5%) receive <strong>Tier 1</strong> and the next 48 (to 20%) receive <strong>Tier 2</strong>.</span>
              </li>
            </ul>

            <div class="example-head">Example B · Team defence badge (illustrative two teams)</div>
            <p class="para">
              Suppose two teams in a division of 198 qualifying teams, one facing many more shots
              than the other.
            </p>
            <ul class="example-rows">
              <li>
                <span class="example-label">Team A (Heavy)</span>
                <span>16.0 shots faced a match. Gave up 24 goals where its opponents usually score 32.0 → record = ln(32 ÷ 24) = <strong>+0.288</strong></span>
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="example-label">Team B (Light)</span>
                <span>12.2 shots faced a match. Gave up 16 goals where its opponents usually score 24.0 → record = ln(24 ÷ 16) = <strong>+0.405</strong></span>
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="example-label">The cut</span>
                <span>ceil(0.20 × 198) = <strong>40</strong> badges, plus ties with the 40th record.</span>
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="example-label">Applying the cut</span>
                <span>If the 40th-best record is +0.35, Team B holds the <strong>team defence badge</strong> and Team A does not, though Team A faced more shots: the badge credits keeping opponents from shooting as well as stopping the shots they take.</span>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </details>
      </section>

      {/* ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
          PRACTICAL REFERENCE · PLAYER SHEET, RELIABILITY & SCHEDULE
          ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */}
      <section class="band" id="sheet">
        <h3 class="sechead">INTERPRETING YOUR PLAYER SHEET</h3>

        <h4 class="subhead">Team figures for regular defenders and goalkeepers</h4>
        <p class="para">
          Every regular defender's and goalkeeper's player sheet prints three team figures for the
          current season, under TEAM DEFENCE. They are the team's facts, not a mark for the player;
          when the team holds the badge, the block says so:
        </p>
        <ol class="list">
          <li>
            <strong>Shots faced a match, with the workload group</strong> (beside the division
            average). Shows which third of the division's teams by shots faced this one sits in.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Shots faced that went in</strong> (beside the division average). The share of
            shots faced that ended in goals.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Goals given up, beside what the opponents usually score.</strong> Printed as one
            sentence — for example, “Gave up 24 goals where its opponents usually score 31.” This
            is the <span class="sym">GA</span> vs. <span class="sym">EGA</span> comparison behind
            the team defence badge.
          </li>
        </ol>

        <h4 class="subhead">The three reasons a sheet reads “Incomplete records”</h4>
        <p class="para">
          “Incomplete records” always means missing or unmatched published data prevented a fair
          comparison — never poor performance. The sheet states which reason applied:
        </p>
        <ul class="list">
          <li><strong>Missing minutes:</strong> box scores omitted minutes for too many matches with no season stats total available (or unprinted matches could have lifted a borderline player over the eligibility bar).</li>
          <li><strong>No position:</strong> neither the roster nor the box scores printed a readable position.</li>
          <li><strong>Unmatched name:</strong> the player's name could not be reliably matched from one box score to the next.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="band" id="reliability">
        <h3 class="sechead">RELIABILITY AND CONSISTENCY</h3>
        <h4 class="question">How consistent are the tier results?</h4>
        <p class="para">
          Small margins matter near any cutoff: a single goal, a handful of shots, or being listed
          as a midfielder rather than a forward can move a player near the 5% or 20% boundary, and a
          single goal conceded can move a team across the badge's.
        </p>
        <p class="para">
          To test how much a tier reflects real season-long performance rather than luck, Touchline
          splits a season's matches randomly into two halves and runs the full tier formula
          independently on each half. Because 20% of eligible players (or teams) receive one, pure chance
          would produce about <strong>2 in 10</strong> repeat holders across halves. Instead:
        </p>
        <ul class="list">
          <li>
            <strong>Tier 1 (shooting and scoring):</strong> about <strong>8 or 9 in 10</strong>{" "}
            hold <em>a</em> tier (Tier 1 or Tier 2) on the other half — about{" "}
            <strong>7 in 10</strong> among men's forwards. (Retaining some tier across halves is
            not the same as retaining Tier 1 specifically.)
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Tier 2 (shooting and scoring):</strong> about <strong>5 in 10</strong> hold a
            tier on the other half.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Team defence badge:</strong> about <strong>5 in 10</strong> holding teams hold
            it on the other half, again against 2 in 10 by chance. It is the team's whole
            defending that repeats: most of it is how few shots the team lets opponents take.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p class="para">
          A full season uses twice as many matches as either half, so published tiers are steadier
          than half-season checks. These tests measure consistency within past box scores — they do
          not predict future performance. The badge has not been tested season forward; a team's
          record correlates about 0.5 to 0.7 with its record the season before.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="band" id="when">
        <h3 class="sechead">WHEN TIERS ARE SET &amp; {division.toUpperCase()} COVERAGE</h3>
        <p class="para">
          Tiers are calculated once a season, in {setIn}, before postseason honours are announced,
          and are not moved afterward. The minutes printed beside a tier are the minutes counted
          through that day.{retroactive && ` ${retroactive}`}{firstSeasonNote && ` ${firstSeasonNote}`}
        </p>
        <p class="para" id="retired">
          Until {retired}, regular defenders and goalkeepers held a <strong>team defence
          tier</strong>: their team's record, compared only with teams facing a similar number of
          shots and averaged over two or three seasons, handed to each of them. A review that day
          found nothing in it that belonged to one player, and the comparison inside workload
          thirds did not repeat. It left player sheets that day on every level, and the team
          defence badge, on the team page, took its place. The shooting and scoring tiers did not
          change.
        </p>
        <p class="para">
          Tiers currently cover {division} men's and women's soccer at the programmes Touchline
          follows.{elsewhere.map((line) => ` ${line}`).join("")}
        </p>
      </section>

    </>)}</article>

    <SiteFooter seasons={seasons} />
  </div>

